"## Deliverable brief — confirmation\n\nI'll confirm before touching any data, since the privacy constraint here is stricter than usual.\n\n**Deliverable:** a single, self-contained `.html` file (no sidecar files, no external requests) that a non-technical reader can open by double-clicking, containing an executive summary, a small set of charts rendered as **inlined base64 images** (PNG or SVG), and plain-language captions. Nothing interactive, nothing that carries a data payload.\n\n**Privacy constraint, stated explicitly in your three layers:**\n\n| Layer | What must be clean |\n|---|---|\n| **Serialized** | No row-level records, no identifier columns, no raw DataFrame dumps, no data embedded in JSON/JS chart specs (this rules out Plotly/Altair/ECharts output — those serialize your data into the file). Only aggregate numbers I deliberately write as text. |\n| **Rendered** | No customer names/emails/IDs in titles, axis labels, legends, tooltips, alt text, filenames, captions, or the HTML `<title>`/meta description. Small or unique cells suppressed so a group can't be reverse-engineered (e.g., a \"region\" with one customer). |\n| **Metadata** | Stripped from the artifact, not just hidden: matplotlib writes `Software: Matplotlib` and sometimes timestamps into PNG chunks; SVG files carry generator strings and can embed the source path. I'll re-serialize every image through PIL with `pnginfo=None` (or explicitly cleared SVG metadata), and keep the source file's path/name out of the document. |\n\n**Assumptions I'm making — tell me which to change:**\n\n1. **Audience** is business/non-technical: they want \"what happened,\" not method. Descriptive statistics only, no forecasting, no model output.\n2. **Format**: one HTML file, opens offline in any modern browser, ~2–4 printed pages, readable on screen and in print/PDF. All CSS and images inlined; no CDN links, since an external fetch can leak a referrer.\n3. **Grain**: aggregates only, at a coarse level (month × one or two dimensions), with a **minimum-cell threshold of 5 records** suppressed by default.\n4. **Identifiers are dropped before aggregation**, not filtered at the end — earliest possible removal.\n5. **Labour split**: I'll assemble the HTML, but the final privacy pass is something you should be able to *verify*, so I'll keep every transformation visible in the notebook rather than doing it in one opaque step.\n\n## What I need from you to proceed\n\n1. **File path/URI** (and format — `.xlsx`, `.csv`, `.parquet`?). I have no file yet, so I can't run anything meaningful until you give me one.\n2. **Which columns are sensitive.** My default: anything matching `name`, `email`, `phone`, `address`, `ip`, `customer_id`, `account_no`, plus any free-text notes field (free text is the most common accidental PII leak — I'd exclude it entirely unless you tell me it's clean).\n3. **The KPIs you want**: revenue, order count, AOV, repeat-purchase rate, units? Anything you specifically need or specifically must not show (e.g., margin)?\n4. **Breakdown dimensions**: region, channel, segment, product category, month?\n5. **Scope and exclusions**: date range, and any test/staff accounts that should be removed.\n6. **Titles and attribution**: report title, and whether to include a brand header. Note that an \"prepared by\" name is itself metadata — I'd suggest a generic title or a team name rather than a person's.\n7. **Suppression threshold and rounding** — I'll default to 5 and to rounding currency to whole units, unless told otherwise.\n\nOnce you point me at the file, my first step will be a schema-only inspection: column names, dtypes, and row count — printing no values — so we can agree on the sensitive-field list before anything is read into a report. Then I'll go aggregate → chart → sanitize → assemble, showing my work at each step.\n\nOne flag on your wording: \"must not contain any residual sensitive information in its serialized, rendered, or metadata forms\" is a strong requirement, and the one place it commonly fails silently is chart images. I'll treat image metadata stripping as a required, verified step rather than a best-effort one — but I can't make the file provably free of re-identification risk from aggregates alone (e.g., a total that matches one known customer's spend). If that's a real concern, tell me and I'll raise the suppression threshold or coarsen the dimensions."